# Who gets asked to review what.
#
# Same rules as two-bot and two-web: handles here are individual GitHub accounts,
# not teams. The TogetherWeOwn org has no teams and one member with write access,
# so a team would be a group of one. Promote to real teams when a second person
# has write access — the path rules below are already split along those lines.
#
# This file is advisory. The org is on the free plan and this repo is private, so
# branch protection and rulesets are unavailable; GitHub cannot require a code
# owner's approval here. It still auto-requests the owner as a reviewer when a PR
# opens, which is the reason to keep it accurate.
#
# A rule naming an account that does not have write access to this repo is
# silently dropped by GitHub. Nothing warns you. That is how this file broke: it
# named teams in the old two-gaming org, none of which exist here.
#
# Earlier revisions pointed at scripts/setup-github.sh in two-bot and its
# docs/GITHUB.md as the source of team truth. Both still describe the old
# two-gaming team model and no longer govern this file.

# Default: the design system.
*                           @Rick7C2

# Tokens are the contract. `resources/css/two.css` in two-web is a copy of
# tokens/two.css, so a change here lands in the live site's colours, spacing and
# type scale.
/tokens/                    @Rick7C2

# The gates that make the accessibility claim mean something — "make CI green" is
# the easiest thing in the repo to get wrong on purpose.
/.github/workflows/         @Rick7C2
/tools/                     @Rick7C2

# Source-control posture. Changing who reviews what, or removing a hook, is not a
# design decision.
/.github/CODEOWNERS         @Rick7C2
/.githooks/                 @Rick7C2
/.gitleaks.toml             @Rick7C2
